latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Identify the colored line at the top of the grid (first row).\n   b. Identify the colored line at the bottom of the grid (last row).\n   c. Find the red line (a row entirely filled with color 2) in the middle of the grid.\n\n2. Determine the overlap between top and bottom colored lines:\n   a. Create sets of column indices for non-black cells in the top and bottom rows.\n   b. Find the intersection of these sets to get the overlapping columns.\n\n3. Calculate the dimensions and position of the yellow rectangle:\n   a. Width: Count the number of overlapping columns.\n   b. Height: From just below the red line to just above the bottom of the grid.\n   c. Starting column: The rightmost column of the overlap.\n   d. Starting row: Just below the red line.\n\n4. Create a new grid based on the input:\n   a. Make a deep copy of the input grid.\n\n5. Draw the yellow rectangle:\n   a. Iterate through the calculated area.\n   b. Set each cell in this area to yellow (color 4).\n\n6. Return the modified grid.\n\nHere's a more detailed breakdown of each step:\n\n1. Analyzing the grid:\n   - Use list comprehension or similar to find non-black cells in the first and last rows.\n   - Iterate through rows to find the all-red row.\n\n2. Finding overlap:\n   - Convert the lists of colored cells to sets.\n   - Use set intersection to find overlapping columns.\n\n3. Calculating rectangle dimensions:\n   - Width = len(overlap)\n   - Height = (grid height - red line row - 1)\n   - Start column = max(overlap)\n   - Start row = red line row + 1\n\n4. Creating new grid:\n   - Use the deep_copy() method of ColoredGrid.\n\n5. Drawing rectangle:\n   - Use nested loops to iterate over the rectangle area.\n   - Use the set_cell() method of ColoredGrid to change colors.\n\n6. Return the modified grid.\n\nThis plan should work for all the given test cases and should be flexible enough to handle variations in input, as long as they follow the same general pattern."
